/*
    SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later

    Runs @DISPLAY_NAME@'s own actions from a screen edge.

    KWin's screen edges run KWin's own actions and nothing else: `rmpr edges`
    configures those, and no amount of configuring them can open a surface this
    shell draws. A shell that wants the pointer at the left of the screen to
    mean something has to register the edge itself, and `registerScreenEdge` is
    the only supported way to do that without a C++ effect.

    Separate from the window-list script on purpose. That one is a read -- it
    never moves, closes or focuses anything -- and mixing an action into it
    would make its header a lie. Two packages also mean the window list can be
    on with no edge claimed, which is the usual case.

    The bindings are rendered in rather than read from a config file. KWin
    scripts can only read their own configuration through a KConfigXT file,
    which is a second place for the same setting to live and drift; a script
    that is re-rendered and reloaded whenever the setting changes has one.

    Like the window list, this can only *call* DBus, never be called, so it
    pushes to the same daemon -- which already knows how to run every action a
    key can be bound to, and runs them detached for the same reason: the edge
    callback is inside the compositor.
*/

const SERVICE = "@DBUS_NAME@";
const PATH = "/Edges";
const IFACE = "@DBUS_NAME@.Edges";

// [[border, "action"], ...] -- KWin's ElectricBorder number and the action
// name the daemon knows. Empty when nothing is bound, which is a script that
// loads, claims no edge and does nothing: `edges shell <edge> none` leaves it
// installed rather than tearing the package down for a setting that is
// usually turned straight back on.
const BINDINGS = @EDGE_BINDINGS@;

// True keeps the edge armed; KWin re-approaches otherwise. There is no second
// meaning to returning false here -- the action has already been asked for by
// the time this returns.
function trigger(action) {
    callDBus(SERVICE, PATH, IFACE, "Triggered", String(action));
    return true;
}

for (let i = 0; i < BINDINGS.length; ++i) {
    const border = BINDINGS[i][0];
    const action = BINDINGS[i][1];
    // The callback is built outside the loop body's scope on purpose: a
    // closure over the loop variable would hand every edge the last action.
    registerScreenEdge(border, (function (bound) {
        return function () { return trigger(bound); };
    })(action));
}
